* The queue manager mc's MQ panes are tried against. setup-wsl.sh feeds this
* to runmqsc after every run, so everything here can be run again: REPLACE
* keeps the messages already on a queue.

DEFINE QLOCAL('DEV.QUEUE.1') REPLACE
DEFINE QLOCAL('DEV.QUEUE.2') REPLACE
DEFINE QLOCAL('DEV.QUEUE.3') REPLACE
DEFINE QLOCAL('DEV.DEAD.LETTER.QUEUE') REPLACE
ALTER QMGR DEADQ('DEV.DEAD.LETTER.QUEUE')

* The client channel mc connects through.
DEFINE CHANNEL('DEV.APP.SVRCONN') CHLTYPE(SVRCONN) TRPTYPE(TCP) MCAUSER(' ') REPLACE

* A new queue manager blocks every administrator (*MQADMIN) on every client
* channel. mcapp is in group mqm, because PCF inquiries and browsing any queue
* need it, so on this one channel only the user 'nobody' is blocked. Fine for
* a test queue manager; not for anything else.
SET CHLAUTH('DEV.APP.SVRCONN') TYPE(BLOCKUSER) USERLIST('nobody') ACTION(REPLACE)

* Every client connection has to give a Linux user and its password.
ALTER AUTHINFO('SYSTEM.DEFAULT.AUTHINFO.IDPWOS') AUTHTYPE(IDPWOS) CHCKCLNT(REQUIRED) ADOPTCTX(YES)
REFRESH SECURITY TYPE(CONNAUTH)

* Started with the queue manager from now on; setup-wsl.sh starts it this once.
DEFINE LISTENER('DEV.LISTENER.TCP') TRPTYPE(TCP) PORT(1414) CONTROL(QMGR) REPLACE
